1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:14,500
Mean

2
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:31,820
The dark images are part of our imagination,

3
00:00:32,620 --> 00:00:35,040
towering, oppressive stone monoliths

4
00:00:35,041 --> 00:00:37,680
where the pious, deformed, broken men

5
00:00:37,681 --> 00:00:41,380
find solace and shelter among the grinning gargoyles.

6
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:57,200
But in reality, Gothic cathedrals are celebrations of the spirit.

7
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:00,700
They are structures of overwhelming strength,

8
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:05,060
designed to teach and inspire through the beauty of their architecture

9
00:01:05,061 --> 00:01:07,420
and remarkable technical virtuosity.

10
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:14,780
Here at Notre Dame in Paris, the genius of Gothic beauty is abundantly clear.

11
00:01:14,781 --> 00:01:23,100
The Gothic architect wanted your eye to ascend toward heaven, drawing your eye upward.

12
00:01:23,101 --> 00:01:28,160
That beautiful arch draws your eye up as high as it can possibly go.

13
00:01:28,161 --> 00:01:35,460
Because people in the Middle Ages really wanted to build these great castles of the spirit,

14
00:01:35,461 --> 00:01:36,900
if you will.

15
00:01:36,901 --> 00:01:41,440
And they believed that a cathedral should be a place of comfort, where you could come

16
00:01:41,441 --> 00:01:47,680
in from the outside, leaving your cares, your worries, your sins behind, and enter into

17
00:01:47,681 --> 00:01:50,120
the presence of God.

18
00:01:50,121 --> 00:01:54,840
That's what they were trying to achieve in the Middle Ages, was a convergence of heaven

19
00:01:54,841 --> 00:01:55,840
on earth.

20
00:01:55,841 --> 00:02:02,980
They wanted to build these great cathedrals to the glory of God, to be, if you will, a

21
00:02:02,981 --> 00:02:06,020
city of God here on earth.

22
00:02:06,021 --> 00:02:09,460
And so there is, in a sense, a convergence.

23
00:02:09,461 --> 00:02:13,600
One is imperfect, but we are created in God's image,

24
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:19,140
and so we do our best to create these beautiful cathedrals

25
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:23,680
to the glory of God in homage to him.

26
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:27,020
But a cathedral is more than an homage to God.

27
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:29,780
It serves a specific purpose.

28
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:32,780
Its name comes from the Greek word kathedra,

29
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:35,820
which means seat, and a cathedral is a church

30
00:02:35,821 --> 00:02:42,720
houses the seat of a bishop. As a center of church power, a cathedral is also a

31
00:02:42,721 --> 00:02:46,500
center of learning and inspiration, demanding an awesome architectural

32
00:02:46,501 --> 00:02:53,520
presence which matches its importance. In the 12th century, that was best

33
00:02:53,521 --> 00:02:59,160
expressed through an innovative new kind of design called Gothic. Originally it was

34
00:02:59,161 --> 00:03:05,280
a style of architecture that in some minds was thought to be rather bizarre

35
00:03:05,281 --> 00:03:12,840
and unusual. Gothic has three basic elements actually. Number one, rib vaulting.

36
00:03:12,841 --> 00:03:18,720
Number two, flying buttresses. And thirdly, and in some ways the most important, the

37
00:03:18,721 --> 00:03:25,400
pointed arch. The concept was a radical departure from early Roman churches,

38
00:03:25,401 --> 00:03:31,280
which were small castle-like structures with small windows and massive walls.

39
00:03:31,281 --> 00:03:38,220
Instead, a Gothic cathedral began with an ambitious floor plan called a cruciform, resembling the shape of the Christian cross.

40
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:46,500
Because great height would eventually rise from this design, construction efforts began with a solid foundation.

41
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:52,740
Layers of stone blocks set 25 feet into the earth to support the building's great weight.

42
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:57,860
Each block needed to be perfectly level and aligned or the entire structure could collapse,

43
00:03:57,861 --> 00:04:00,000
as was the case in Bouvre, France,

44
00:04:00,340 --> 00:04:02,940
where the pieces of a 1284 A.D. cathedral

45
00:04:02,941 --> 00:04:04,260
which fell during construction

46
00:04:04,261 --> 00:04:07,120
are a haunting reminder of imperfect masonry.

47
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:10,000
Today, only a section of the building remains,

48
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:12,260
too heavy and cumbersome to be disassembled

49
00:04:12,261 --> 00:04:13,580
and rebuilt as intended.

50
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:18,780
It might appear that grand granite pillars

51
00:04:18,781 --> 00:04:21,700
are providing a cathedral's structural support,

52
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:25,000
but actually it is the walls which hold the building up.

53
00:04:25,001 --> 00:04:33,360
In earlier times, this would necessitate very thick and solid sides, but the Gothic aesthetic

54
00:04:33,361 --> 00:04:37,215
demands an innovation never before possible in giant structures.

55
00:04:37,216 --> 00:04:38,420
Enormous expanses of

56
00:04:38,421 --> 00:04:44,343
glass embedded in the walls to shower the inside with a holy light.

57
00:04:44,344 --> 00:04:46,200
Cutting away sections

58
00:04:46,201 --> 00:04:50,651
of the stone walls, however, reduced the overall stability of the building.

59
00:04:50,652 --> 00:04:51,600
The solution was

60
00:04:51,601 --> 00:04:58,940
an ingenious 12th century notion to support or buttress the construction.

61
00:04:58,941 --> 00:05:02,900
Traditionally a building's roof is supported by its walls.

62
00:05:02,901 --> 00:05:08,920
In early structures, entrances into a building evolved into arches and load-bearing columns.

63
00:05:08,921 --> 00:05:15,280
But as wooden roofs were replaced by fireproof stone ceilings called vaults, walls became

64
00:05:15,281 --> 00:05:21,680
thicker to support the increased weight and resist the outward thrust.

65
00:05:21,681 --> 00:05:25,660
In a refinement of the principle, the downward weight of the ceiling was directed to specific

66
00:05:25,661 --> 00:05:30,260
points over the columns which were thickened for the additional load.

67
00:05:30,261 --> 00:05:34,500
The result was a building which resembled a ribbed skeleton, and it allowed the walls

68
00:05:34,501 --> 00:05:39,520
to be opened even farther for larger windows.

69
00:05:39,521 --> 00:05:44,320
But as cathedrals grew larger and taller, the structure's weight inside and the force

70
00:05:44,321 --> 00:05:46,800
of the wind outside increased.

71
00:05:46,801 --> 00:05:49,240
To counteract these two unstable forces

72
00:05:49,241 --> 00:05:51,360
without thickening the walls and reducing the size

73
00:05:51,361 --> 00:05:53,620
of the windows, additional support

74
00:05:53,621 --> 00:05:58,020
was placed outside the interior space.

75
00:05:58,021 --> 00:06:01,080
The result was a row of buttresses connected to the walls

76
00:06:01,081 --> 00:06:05,140
by stone arms called flying buttresses.

77
00:06:05,141 --> 00:06:07,040
Here, the downward thrust of the ceiling

78
00:06:07,041 --> 00:06:09,320
pushes against the wall, then travels

79
00:06:09,321 --> 00:06:12,080
across the expanse of the flying buttresses

80
00:06:12,081 --> 00:06:16,700
and is channeled through the outside buttress columns to the foundation below.

81
00:06:18,980 --> 00:06:22,100
Most buttresses are topped by an ornate pinnacle.

82
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:26,480
These festive stone designs are not simply to enhance the beauty of the structure.

83
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:31,180
It is their weight which helps the buttresses perform their task.

84
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:36,460
You ended up with very thin, very tall, very thin walls,

85
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:40,760
which were made of stones piled up.

86
00:06:40,761 --> 00:06:46,880
So the buttresses stopped the whole building from pushing out, and the vault formed it.

87
00:06:48,300 --> 00:06:52,940
A vault is the architectural name for the unique ceiling construction of a Gothic cathedral,

88
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:55,540
and part of the genius behind its design.

89
00:06:56,800 --> 00:07:00,880
First, temporary wooden centerings are mounted on scaffolding.

90
00:07:01,160 --> 00:07:05,280
Then, cut stones are laid in place from the bottom up and mortared together,

91
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:09,100
though it is their own downward weight which keep them firmly set together.

92
00:07:10,060 --> 00:07:12,640
Finally, a keystone completes the assembly,

93
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:15,500
so when the temporary centering are removed,

94
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:18,480
only the stone frame of the ceiling remains.

95
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:22,560
The expanse of the vaulting within the frame

96
00:07:22,561 --> 00:07:25,080
begins with a webbing of very lightweight stone

97
00:07:25,081 --> 00:07:27,720
mortared together with a layer of wood planking underneath.

98
00:07:28,660 --> 00:07:29,840
Once the mortar is set,

99
00:07:29,841 --> 00:07:33,380
a four-inch layer of concrete is poured over the entire area

100
00:07:33,381 --> 00:07:34,780
to stabilize the structure.

101
00:07:34,781 --> 00:07:42,420
the process is simply repeated until the entire ceiling area has been completed

102
00:07:42,421 --> 00:07:47,940
and because the building and its ceiling are primarily made of stone there is little chance

103
00:07:47,941 --> 00:07:53,180
of fire damage a primary concern in an age when most secular buildings were made of wood

104
00:07:53,181 --> 00:08:02,520
i think you can say that it is an act of faith it has to do with gravity it has to do with the

105
00:08:02,521 --> 00:08:03,860
thrust of weight.

106
00:08:03,861 --> 00:08:10,480
When you build a Gothic cathedral, you start at the bottom, laying stone upon stone,

107
00:08:10,481 --> 00:08:16,977
course upon course, and then you pray. But as I say, it works.

108
00:08:16,978 --> 00:08:20,120
Using such heavy and ponderous

109
00:08:20,121 --> 00:08:25,560
materials in constructions, it seems a miracle that Gothic cathedrals would be homes of holy light.

110
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:29,282
But that was their central and original intent.

111
00:08:29,283 --> 00:08:32,260
We sort of glom on to a date for the beginning

112
00:08:32,261 --> 00:08:38,725
of Gothic. We think of the Abbot Souget in France who redid his abbey in 1130.

113
00:08:38,726 --> 00:08:40,300
And he in many ways

114
00:08:40,301 --> 00:08:45,840
is known as the father of stained glass because he believed that stained glass let in divine light.

115
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:52,840
Natural light coming through colored glass in a sense was divine and created sort of a mystery.

116
00:08:53,720 --> 00:08:57,461
And so the stained glass windows were very, very important.

117
00:08:57,462 --> 00:08:59,680
And coming to a Gothic cathedral is

118
00:08:59,681 --> 00:09:02,852
really a visible experience. It's visual.

119
00:09:02,853 --> 00:09:07,340
You see, and it is through your sight that you are able to

120
00:09:07,341 --> 00:09:10,480
interpret often the meaning of your faith.

121
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:25,060
Religion acts on faith, and sometimes faith is bolstered by relics, objects believed to have been

122
00:09:25,061 --> 00:09:29,200
handled by saints or even decaying pieces of the saints themselves.

123
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:34,380
Most of these relics have long since perished,

124
00:09:34,381 --> 00:09:37,960
and there is great debate as to whether these relics ever existed at all.

125
00:09:39,380 --> 00:09:43,240
At Chartres Cathedral, 60 miles south of the modern city of Paris,

126
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:45,480
one of the world's most important Gothic structures

127
00:09:45,481 --> 00:09:50,280
was built to house such a relic, the shawl of the Virgin Mary.

128
00:09:50,281 --> 00:09:58,880
so it's here that mary the queen of all the saints would intercede for humanity most favorably

129
00:09:58,881 --> 00:10:04,140
because this is where her sacred relic is kept that explains the size of this great building on

130
00:10:04,141 --> 00:10:11,040
a clear day like this can be seen for 30 40 miles our modern visitors drive or come in buses or

131
00:10:11,041 --> 00:10:16,020
trains the medieval pilgrim walked here imagine you could see it then for three or four days

132
00:10:16,021 --> 00:10:17,905
getting closer and closer.

133
00:10:17,906 --> 00:10:22,980
1194, the great fire destroyed much of the city and the preceding 11th

134
00:10:22,981 --> 00:10:26,952
century church, which stood behind me, was badly damaged.

135
00:10:26,953 --> 00:10:29,460
At first it was feared that the holy

136
00:10:29,461 --> 00:10:35,652
relic, given to shout by King Charles the Bald in 876, had burnt in the fire.

137
00:10:35,653 --> 00:10:37,260
But three days after

138
00:10:37,261 --> 00:10:42,740
the fire, we're told that it was brought out to the crypts unharmed, and this was proclaimed a

139
00:10:42,741 --> 00:10:48,760
miraculous sign from Mary that she desired a more praiseworthy church and she got it.

140
00:10:48,761 --> 00:10:53,090
We do not know who built Chartres Cathedral. I know this is strange.

141
00:10:53,091 --> 00:10:55,000
We know who built the pyramids

142
00:10:55,001 --> 00:11:00,360
or the Parthenon but not 13th century Chartres Cathedral or how many master masons were involved

143
00:11:00,361 --> 00:11:04,860
or who the craftsmen were, who sculpted John the Baptist in the north door behind me or

144
00:11:04,861 --> 00:11:08,007
God creating Adam. They didn't sign their work.

145
00:11:08,008 --> 00:11:11,220
What is known about the construction of Chartres

146
00:11:11,221 --> 00:11:13,660
are the people who helped fund the effort.

147
00:11:14,380 --> 00:11:17,360
The giant stained glass windows acknowledge local merchants

148
00:11:17,361 --> 00:11:18,700
who sponsored the art.

149
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:22,940
Instead of their names, they are identified by images of themselves

150
00:11:22,941 --> 00:11:25,720
performing the crafts which raised the money.

151
00:11:28,200 --> 00:11:32,000
Like all Gothic cathedrals, the exterior design of Chartres

152
00:11:32,001 --> 00:11:34,980
is meant to be an illustrated history for the faithful

153
00:11:34,981 --> 00:11:37,220
who were unable to decipher the written word.

154
00:11:38,220 --> 00:11:42,180
Instead, three-dimensional figures depict great theological notions.

155
00:11:44,700 --> 00:11:49,620
Thousands of details, tens of thousands, in fact, in the sculpture on the outside.

156
00:11:49,860 --> 00:11:52,160
In fact, we can read Shark Cathedral like a book.

157
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:55,380
It's a long story. The beginning is behind me.

158
00:11:55,940 --> 00:12:01,700
The creation cycle, the story of Adam and Eve and paradise was lost.

159
00:12:02,300 --> 00:12:05,160
In the outer archivalts of the central bay of the North Door,

160
00:12:05,161 --> 00:12:08,085
where we're standing, two archivalts.

161
00:12:08,086 --> 00:12:11,800
On the outside, God creates, and on the inside,

162
00:12:11,940 --> 00:12:14,242
the things that are being created.

163
00:12:14,243 --> 00:12:18,100
So if you start at the bottom, God created, and there are

164
00:12:18,101 --> 00:12:23,140
inscriptions in Latin, created the heaven and the earth, meaning God created all things.

165
00:12:23,920 --> 00:12:30,060
The first day is the second up. God created the day and night. Night is blindfolded.

166
00:12:30,061 --> 00:12:32,580
Day leads night by the hand.

167
00:12:33,040 --> 00:12:33,840
Note that personified.

168
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:39,720
Next, God separated the firmaments and created fire, earth, water and air.

169
00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:42,220
Next, he created the plants.

170
00:12:42,920 --> 00:12:44,620
Then the sun, the moon and the planets.

171
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:46,640
Then the birds and the fish.

172
00:12:47,620 --> 00:12:50,360
And when you come to shout, I should add, including pigeons.

173
00:12:51,740 --> 00:12:54,400
Next, God created the animals.

174
00:12:55,400 --> 00:12:56,560
You might see a lion.

175
00:12:57,200 --> 00:12:58,320
Then some more trees.

176
00:12:58,321 --> 00:13:01,607
the Garden of Eden, sometimes referred to as Salem.

177
00:13:01,608 --> 00:13:04,120
American audiences will know that word.

178
00:13:04,220 --> 00:13:10,905
Many American towns are named Salem, Jerusalem, Salem, Shalom. The word means peace.

179
00:13:10,906 --> 00:13:11,940
So the Garden

180
00:13:11,941 --> 00:13:13,484
of Eden was a place of peace.

181
00:13:13,485 --> 00:13:16,520
Then a very remarkable detail, difficult to find when you

182
00:13:16,521 --> 00:13:20,919
come to show up, but the very top on the outside, God creating Adam.

183
00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:22,860
Adam is kneeling with his head

184
00:13:22,861 --> 00:13:27,420
on God's knee, and God is caressing Adam's head gently, always like a divine potter

185
00:13:27,421 --> 00:13:29,600
fashioning Adam's head out of clay.

186
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:34,280
It is a mistake to consider the iconography of a Gothic cathedral

187
00:13:34,281 --> 00:13:37,040
as intimidating and moralistic storytelling.

188
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:40,480
There is a lightness and joy to the figures,

189
00:13:40,481 --> 00:13:43,020
which has been lost to the ages.

190
00:13:44,920 --> 00:13:47,800
Originally, these exterior sculptures were hand-painted

191
00:13:47,801 --> 00:13:49,320
in bright luminous colors.

192
00:13:50,300 --> 00:13:52,180
Over the years, the paint has worn away,

193
00:13:52,540 --> 00:13:54,600
and later generations have become more accustomed

194
00:13:54,601 --> 00:13:56,840
to the gray figures we see today.

195
00:13:57,420 --> 00:14:07,540
Perhaps the most striking features of Gothic cathedrals are the architectural devices used to divert rainwater from the roof away from the sides of the building.

196
00:14:07,541 --> 00:14:16,280
They are called gargoyles, and their hollow form is a funnel which allows water to spill from their mouths with a distinctive gargling sound.

197
00:14:17,780 --> 00:14:22,340
A gargoyle, strictly speaking, comes from the word gargoy in French.

198
00:14:22,540 --> 00:14:26,340
Gargoyer is given as an English gargle and gargle, water, you see.

199
00:14:26,341 --> 00:14:29,100
so a gargoyle is a water spout.

200
00:14:29,420 --> 00:14:30,540
You can see others around the building.

201
00:14:31,820 --> 00:14:35,460
Many Gothic cathedrals are also festooned with grotesques,

202
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:39,840
equally hideous creatures who both frighten evil spirits away from the sanctuary

203
00:14:39,841 --> 00:14:43,780
and peer longingly at the pious congregations below.

204
00:14:45,360 --> 00:14:47,740
Grotesques serve no practical function,

205
00:14:48,280 --> 00:14:53,060
yet they exert a powerful hold on the imagination of most people who visit Gothic cathedrals.

206
00:14:53,061 --> 00:15:03,540
their exaggerated expressions mirror our ecstatic love of god their sad countenance speaks to our

207
00:15:03,541 --> 00:15:10,140
sense of humility before a higher power their melancholy postures remind us of how far we have

208
00:15:10,141 --> 00:15:12,400
strayed from the garden of paradise

209
00:15:23,060 --> 00:15:27,440
It is the sixth largest cathedral in the world,

210
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:30,920
surpassing the size of Chartres and Notre Dame in Paris.

211
00:15:31,860 --> 00:15:34,740
But unlike classic Gothic structures,

212
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:37,500
the National Cathedral in Washington, D.C.

213
00:15:37,501 --> 00:15:41,000
was born of the passions of laymen and not of the clergy.

214
00:15:42,540 --> 00:15:45,760
In 1891, Charles Glover Gathers,

215
00:15:46,200 --> 00:15:47,540
a prominent Washington banker,

216
00:15:47,541 --> 00:15:50,080
promoted the idea of a monumental cathedral

217
00:15:50,081 --> 00:15:52,820
as a moral center within the nation's capital.

218
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:59,060
Two years later, Congress granted a charter to the Protestant Episcopal Cathedral Foundation,

219
00:15:59,061 --> 00:16:04,060
and the search was on for a member of the clergy to lead the construction effort.

220
00:16:04,061 --> 00:16:11,060
The Rite Reverend Henry Yates Satterley was elected the cathedral's first bishop in 1896,

221
00:16:11,061 --> 00:16:14,060
without a building or a congregation to lead.

222
00:16:14,061 --> 00:16:20,060
But Satterley had a vision which would propel the project for the next century.

223
00:16:20,061 --> 00:16:25,340
He began by choosing a site for the building which would command the best view of the city.

224
00:16:26,420 --> 00:16:29,000
Back when this site was selected, this was all woods,

225
00:16:29,140 --> 00:16:31,600
and this was, I guess, what one called the boondocks.

226
00:16:31,860 --> 00:16:32,880
This was out of town.

227
00:16:33,360 --> 00:16:37,400
Generally speaking, this is considered to be the highest site in Washington.

228
00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:39,840
That's why he selected that, to be seen from downtown.

229
00:16:41,220 --> 00:16:43,180
Satterley literally took the high ground,

230
00:16:43,181 --> 00:16:48,180
and with it, a philosophy for a cathedral which would be facilitated by Episcopalians,

231
00:16:48,181 --> 00:16:51,200
yet transcend all barriers of denomination.

232
00:16:52,220 --> 00:16:56,500
This would be an American church embracing all faiths.

233
00:16:57,440 --> 00:17:01,040
The design would come from George Frederick Bosley,

234
00:17:01,240 --> 00:17:04,040
the greatest Victorian gothicist of his time,

235
00:17:04,041 --> 00:17:07,040
who understood the enduring emotional impact

236
00:17:07,041 --> 00:17:08,980
that Gothic architecture bestows.

237
00:17:10,720 --> 00:17:14,360
September 29, 1907, President Teddy Roosevelt

238
00:17:14,361 --> 00:17:16,820
participates in the laying of the cornerstone,

239
00:17:16,821 --> 00:17:22,060
composed of granite and smaller rocks harvested by pilgrims in the River Jordan.

240
00:17:23,380 --> 00:17:26,340
Over 10,000 people gathered to watch the ceremony.

241
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:30,960
So many people, in fact, that local trolley lines broke under the strain.

242
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:34,060
Important members of Congress and the Supreme Court

243
00:17:34,061 --> 00:17:38,600
stood by the Episcopalian House of Bishops and watched from grandstand seats.

244
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:42,140
Their support was more spiritual than functional.

245
00:17:42,141 --> 00:17:49,140
From the very outset, this cathedral never has received any funds from the federal government,

246
00:17:49,141 --> 00:17:55,140
and we have not ever received any church money, because the National Episcopal Church is not

247
00:17:55,141 --> 00:17:55,841
supported.

248
00:17:55,842 --> 00:18:01,140
So this cathedral is an independent entity, an independent unity, and the money

249
00:18:01,141 --> 00:18:03,466
has been raised that way over the years.

250
00:18:03,467 --> 00:18:06,140
At any one time, the number of people involved

251
00:18:06,141 --> 00:18:11,140
in this cathedral are not what you would expect. That's a small number. The average

252
00:18:11,141 --> 00:18:16,140
The average crew was ran around 30 to 35, 40 men,

253
00:18:16,680 --> 00:18:19,480
masons, laborers, carpenters, and helpers.

254
00:18:20,620 --> 00:18:23,880
And of course we had the architects and their team.

255
00:18:23,881 --> 00:18:25,860
We had the stone carvers.

256
00:18:27,380 --> 00:18:29,860
And then there's the stained glass window makers,

257
00:18:31,560 --> 00:18:33,200
the wrought iron workers and all.

258
00:18:41,140 --> 00:18:47,580
The mechanics of building a Gothic cathedral

259
00:18:47,581 --> 00:18:50,040
have not changed in over 900 years.

260
00:18:53,720 --> 00:18:57,620
Techniques used during the later stages of the work in the 1980s

261
00:18:57,621 --> 00:19:00,520
mirrors the way it was performed in 1907

262
00:19:00,521 --> 00:19:02,720
and, of course, in the 12th century.

263
00:19:04,060 --> 00:19:07,240
We still are laying the stones in a full bed of mortar

264
00:19:07,241 --> 00:19:09,300
using pretty much the traditional tools,

265
00:19:09,301 --> 00:19:13,430
the trowels, the hammers, the mallets. That part hasn't changed.

266
00:19:13,431 --> 00:19:15,560
Of course, the tools that we use,

267
00:19:15,640 --> 00:19:21,560
such as electric hammers and electric drills and air compressors and things like that make our

268
00:19:21,561 --> 00:19:27,100
life much easier, but we're still basically accomplishing the same things with those tools

269
00:19:27,101 --> 00:19:30,069
that the ancient stonemasons were doing.

270
00:19:30,070 --> 00:19:33,780
The stones were then hoisted up to the towers by a

271
00:19:33,781 --> 00:19:36,151
tower crane that we had up there.

272
00:19:36,152 --> 00:19:39,960
From there they were landed on the wall and once they

273
00:19:39,961 --> 00:19:45,320
were landed on the wall we proceeded to pick the stones up with our own lifting devices

274
00:19:45,321 --> 00:19:51,718
that we had built on top of our work area. We used pulleys and chain hoists.

275
00:19:51,719 --> 00:19:52,560
The stones

276
00:19:52,561 --> 00:19:59,120
are laid on a full bed of mortar and each stone is individually beat into place. We

277
00:19:59,121 --> 00:20:03,960
We use the word beating the stone into place using large rawhide-tipped hammers.

278
00:20:04,380 --> 00:20:07,700
There is no steel skeleton that's wrapped in stone.

279
00:20:07,820 --> 00:20:10,860
The stone that you see on the cathedral is the structure.

280
00:20:12,240 --> 00:20:16,240
One cubic foot of limestone weighs nearly 150 pounds.

281
00:20:16,840 --> 00:20:22,440
The combined weight of all the limestone used here exceeds 300 million pounds.

282
00:20:23,200 --> 00:20:27,260
But limestone is a porous material, and like classic European cathedrals,

283
00:20:27,261 --> 00:20:32,380
gargoyles are a key structural element to protect the rock from moisture damage.

284
00:20:33,180 --> 00:20:39,300
Our cathedral is made of limestone and so it's a porous stone and it will retain moisture.

285
00:20:40,020 --> 00:20:45,580
So the gargoyles and grotesque prevent this moisture from seeping into the walls.

286
00:20:46,320 --> 00:20:52,360
Perhaps the most dynamic Gothic influence on the cathedral can be seen in the stained glass windows,

287
00:20:52,361 --> 00:20:54,220
more than 100 of them.

288
00:20:54,221 --> 00:20:58,180
And there was a point at which God said, let there be light.

289
00:20:58,840 --> 00:21:04,040
And this window tells that story in a very modern, abstract way,

290
00:21:04,280 --> 00:21:08,680
with pieces of stained glass made not just a flat glass, by the way,

291
00:21:08,760 --> 00:21:13,280
but made a faceted glass, picking up the light from many different angles

292
00:21:13,281 --> 00:21:18,240
so that all times of the day, in all climates, all types of weather,

293
00:21:18,720 --> 00:21:22,020
you have this beautiful jewel-like quality of that window

294
00:21:22,021 --> 00:21:25,200
which reflects itself down the entire nave of the cathedral.

295
00:21:26,100 --> 00:21:30,260
A window is made first from the inspiration of the artist,

296
00:21:30,900 --> 00:21:33,840
then he actually prepares the window in panels

297
00:21:33,841 --> 00:21:36,360
and often is assisted by a fabricator

298
00:21:36,361 --> 00:21:39,360
who is an expert on doing just that.

299
00:21:39,620 --> 00:21:42,680
The execution of the window takes quite a while,

300
00:21:42,980 --> 00:21:47,700
but the actual installation can be done in a short period of time.

301
00:21:48,020 --> 00:21:51,100
We want our pilgrims, if you will,

302
00:21:51,101 --> 00:21:54,040
to know that this cathedral represents the times

303
00:21:54,041 --> 00:21:56,920
in which you and I are living today, the 20th century.

304
00:21:57,520 --> 00:22:01,040
And a window which does this very well is the space window.

305
00:22:01,960 --> 00:22:05,120
In that window is a very special piece of rock

306
00:22:05,121 --> 00:22:07,220
brought from the surface of the moon.

307
00:22:08,060 --> 00:22:09,680
So that in itself is a reminder.

308
00:22:10,520 --> 00:22:11,680
In that window it says,

309
00:22:12,360 --> 00:22:14,580
Is not God in the height of heaven?

310
00:22:15,580 --> 00:22:18,360
Those words were spoken originally by Job in the Bible

311
00:22:18,361 --> 00:22:21,560
and repeated by Neil Armstrong as he orbited the moon.

312
00:22:23,160 --> 00:22:27,580
The bell tower is officially known as the Gloria and Excelsis Tower.

313
00:22:28,220 --> 00:22:31,180
It rises in the sky 301 feet.

314
00:22:31,740 --> 00:22:34,220
It has two sets of bells in it.

315
00:22:34,720 --> 00:22:38,880
It has a 53-bell carillon, which is played by a carillon or.

316
00:22:48,360 --> 00:22:57,800
And above that is a bell ringing chamber, which holds ten bells.

317
00:22:57,801 --> 00:23:04,300
And these are actually pulled by ropes by bell ringers who are trained in this art.

318
00:23:04,301 --> 00:23:08,720
We ring the bells regularly here at the cathedral.

319
00:23:08,721 --> 00:23:13,860
Bell ringing is an ancient art, both in terms of melody and manufacture of the individual

320
00:23:13,861 --> 00:23:21,960
bells which range in weight from 15 pounds to several tons. Bell makers begin with a

321
00:23:21,961 --> 00:23:27,740
clay model of the proposed shape, then cover it with a thick layer of wax whose depth matches

322
00:23:27,741 --> 00:23:29,783
the dimensions of the finished bell.

323
00:23:29,784 --> 00:23:32,620
The mold is covered with additional layers of clay

324
00:23:32,621 --> 00:23:35,053
and plaster then heated over a fire.

325
00:23:35,054 --> 00:23:38,500
The wax runs out and leaves a cavity into which the

326
00:23:38,501 --> 00:23:40,100
molten bronze is poured.

327
00:23:41,180 --> 00:23:43,780
When the metal cools, the mold is broken

328
00:23:43,781 --> 00:23:46,180
and the bell is smoothed and tuned.

329
00:23:47,280 --> 00:23:50,340
It is important that an extended cooling process

330
00:23:50,341 --> 00:23:52,980
be employed to ensure a perfect tone.

331
00:23:53,780 --> 00:23:57,280
Then an expert bell master must supervise the installation.

332
00:23:58,920 --> 00:24:02,160
Achieving the exact rhythm between ten separate musicians,

333
00:24:02,161 --> 00:24:05,420
each pulling a bell cord, requires exhaustive practice.

334
00:24:06,280 --> 00:24:08,220
Their choreography is called changes.

335
00:24:08,500 --> 00:24:16,400
and is often performed with stays, holding still the bell clappers in order to avoid the deafening ringing during lengthy rehearsals.

336
00:24:17,420 --> 00:24:25,560
When the stays are removed and the bells are allowed to sing, their glorious peal seems to announce a new hope.

337
00:24:26,600 --> 00:24:35,320
This is the sound of a Gothic cathedral, as strong as the stone which defines its form and the people who define its spirit.

338
00:24:38,500 --> 00:24:51,580
The secret to carving stone is to imagine the beautiful figure trapped in the rock and

339
00:24:51,581 --> 00:24:56,500
have the compassion to set it free.

340
00:24:56,501 --> 00:25:01,900
Over the last four decades, Vincent Palumbo has dedicated his artistic compassion to the

341
00:25:01,901 --> 00:25:03,740
glory of the National Cathedral.

342
00:25:03,741 --> 00:25:06,740
Tricks of the trade.

343
00:25:06,741 --> 00:25:09,740
We call it in Italian, we call it

344
00:25:12,740 --> 00:25:15,740
So that's how we build the cathedral,

345
00:25:15,741 --> 00:25:18,740
chiseling away little by little.

346
00:25:18,741 --> 00:25:23,740
All of a sudden, you see, wow, we did that.

347
00:25:23,741 --> 00:25:26,740
We did that.

348
00:25:26,741 --> 00:25:39,740
We have 320 of those little angels that go around the two towers and everyone play music.

349
00:25:39,741 --> 00:25:43,740
They have a different instrument, musical instrument.

350
00:25:43,741 --> 00:25:50,740
This guy, he sounded tambourine, you know, it must be either Italian or Spanish, you know, with tambourine.

351
00:25:50,741 --> 00:25:58,260
but every other one she got the balalaika trombone from back everything everything

352
00:25:58,261 --> 00:26:01,940
everything with the musical instrument everyone is a musical instrument

353
00:26:04,340 --> 00:26:11,540
somewhere under the din of steel and stone there is a music vincent hears an inspiration a revelation

354
00:26:11,541 --> 00:26:17,700
a lyrical prayer which is articulated through the rhythmic process of his hand-wrought art

355
00:26:17,701 --> 00:26:24,020
I got tools, see, we have tools smaller like this, look, look at this.

356
00:26:24,240 --> 00:26:31,240
This, my father used this tools, this specific chisel, my father used this.

357
00:26:32,040 --> 00:26:37,780
I have tools that my grandfather used, so that's traditional.

358
00:26:38,220 --> 00:26:43,780
But you need hundreds of tools besides you, because you're doing something all of a sudden,

359
00:26:43,781 --> 00:26:47,140
you know, you need that shape, look around and use that.

360
00:26:47,141 --> 00:26:50,820
My father died over here while he worked at the cathedral.

361
00:26:52,100 --> 00:26:57,680
Not for accident, for natural cost, but he was working here when he died.

362
00:26:59,060 --> 00:27:03,340
That's one of the reasons I stay so long here at the cathedral,

363
00:27:03,540 --> 00:27:05,800
because it's sentimental reason.

364
00:27:06,660 --> 00:27:10,180
In so many years, in 34 years, I have other requests,

365
00:27:10,181 --> 00:27:15,400
but I was not interested in my life being here at the cathedral.

366
00:27:15,401 --> 00:27:19,400
Now you look at it, I'm the only one left.

367
00:27:19,401 --> 00:27:25,400
Carving needed to be done because that's the way we're going to complete the cathedral.

368
00:27:25,401 --> 00:27:29,400
The cathedral is finished, but it's not complete.

369
00:27:29,401 --> 00:27:35,400
We've got another maybe 60 or 70 years of carving to do, at least.

370
00:27:35,401 --> 00:27:40,400
To the casual observer, a Gothic cathedral appears to be built of stone,

371
00:27:40,401 --> 00:27:45,140
adorned with iron and punctuated by the hard crystal colors of stained glass.

372
00:27:45,141 --> 00:27:51,620
But the sculpted textures of Vincent Palumbo embedded in its form suggest a

373
00:27:51,621 --> 00:27:56,300
structure assembled from stronger materials, the spirit of the human's

374
00:27:56,301 --> 00:28:00,140
passionate longing to celebrate the glory of God.

375
00:28:00,141 --> 00:28:06,320
You work so long you feel you are a part of the building. That's how it is.

376
00:28:06,321 --> 00:28:08,320
See, it's a beautiful building.

377
00:28:08,321 --> 00:28:10,320
Everyone works of it.

378
00:28:10,321 --> 00:28:12,320
We are proud to be part of that.

379
00:28:12,321 --> 00:28:16,320
We can tell our grandchildren, you know,

380
00:28:16,321 --> 00:28:18,320
that's what your grandfather did.

381
00:28:28,320 --> 00:28:30,320
Cathedrals are like glorious machines

382
00:28:30,321 --> 00:28:32,320
which drive the mechanics of the faithful.

383
00:28:32,321 --> 00:28:35,320
Their magnificent height and holy light

384
00:28:35,321 --> 00:28:38,020
are more than expressions of awe.

385
00:28:38,021 --> 00:28:40,120
The structure's exuberant spirit reinforces

386
00:28:40,121 --> 00:28:43,660
the sense of holiness within ourselves.

387
00:28:43,661 --> 00:28:46,260
That is its function.

388
00:28:46,261 --> 00:28:47,860
The performance test of a cathedral

389
00:28:47,861 --> 00:28:50,600
is its resonance in the community.

390
00:28:50,601 --> 00:28:52,540
The National Cathedral is a living legacy

391
00:28:52,541 --> 00:28:56,780
of profound impact upon the character of Washington, D.C.,

392
00:28:56,781 --> 00:28:59,720
and the nation.

393
00:28:59,721 --> 00:29:02,040
Although facilitated by Episcopalians,

394
00:29:02,041 --> 00:29:04,720
the Gothic structure has also been a temporary

395
00:29:04,721 --> 00:29:12,000
house of prayer for two synagogues and hosts services for the Greek Orthodox Church, Baptists,

396
00:29:12,001 --> 00:29:14,882
Roman Catholics, and Charismatic.

397
00:29:14,883 --> 00:29:20,560
Our services include preachers from just about every denomination

398
00:29:20,561 --> 00:29:25,299
within Christianity, as well as Jewish and Islamic speakers.

399
00:29:25,300 --> 00:29:28,300
So its vision and its purpose is to be

400
00:29:28,301 --> 00:29:31,620
a great house of prayer for all people.

401
00:29:32,180 --> 00:29:35,580
There are seven great faiths that come together at this cathedral

402
00:29:35,581 --> 00:29:38,760
to talk about how they can work together

403
00:29:38,761 --> 00:29:42,380
to heal the social ills in our society

404
00:29:42,381 --> 00:29:45,720
and to stand against religious bigotry.

405
00:29:46,780 --> 00:29:51,120
More than 1,500 religious services are performed here every year.

406
00:29:51,820 --> 00:29:54,600
2,000 people a day, more than a half million annually,

407
00:29:54,601 --> 00:29:55,660
visit the cathedral.

408
00:29:56,520 --> 00:30:00,620
Here, the faithful from every level of society celebrate together.

409
00:30:02,900 --> 00:30:08,340
It's true that we do attract a great number of international leaders,

410
00:30:08,600 --> 00:30:11,760
both political as well as religious leaders.

411
00:30:12,620 --> 00:30:15,960
I believe that they are drawn to this cathedral

412
00:30:15,961 --> 00:30:20,860
because it is a sign to them of the American religious experience.

413
00:30:20,861 --> 00:30:31,680
I think the cathedral is that kind of place that represents a national moral conscience.

414
00:30:32,540 --> 00:30:39,340
When musicians against nuclear arms needed to speak out against growing atomic stockpiles in the 1970s,

415
00:30:39,600 --> 00:30:45,000
it was here they sponsored a performance by Leonard Bernstein which focused public attention on the situation.

416
00:30:45,001 --> 00:30:54,000
When America mourned the tragic loss of life in Vietnam, it was here the nation came together to grieve.

417
00:30:54,001 --> 00:31:00,000
For ten years, this nation struggled in Southeast Asia.

418
00:31:00,001 --> 00:31:08,000
2,700,000 American men and women participated in the armed forces in that struggle.

419
00:31:08,001 --> 00:31:13,150
struggle and 57,939 did not return.

420
00:31:13,151 --> 00:31:20,360
It was a day and night service in which the names

421
00:31:20,361 --> 00:31:23,200
of the Vietnam War dead were read aloud.

422
00:31:23,201 --> 00:31:35,000
Paul B. Abbott, Raymond L. Abbott, Robert E. Abbott Jr. Robert W. Abbott, Stephen G.

423
00:31:35,001 --> 00:31:39,730
have it. And when America's struggle for civil rights found a national hero in Dr.

424
00:31:39,731 --> 00:31:40,480
Martin Luther

425
00:31:40,481 --> 00:31:45,220
King Jr., it was the National Cathedral which offered him a pulpit from which to preach his

426
00:31:45,221 --> 00:31:49,112
message of peace to Washington in 1968.

427
00:31:49,113 --> 00:31:55,000
It was a very exciting time for the cathedral staff and for

428
00:31:55,001 --> 00:32:03,020
the city because many of the cathedral staff had gone to the south, particularly Selma, to march

429
00:32:03,021 --> 00:32:06,540
and to participate in the civil rights movement.

430
00:32:07,980 --> 00:32:11,840
It was also important because of what Martin Luther King had to say.

431
00:32:12,680 --> 00:32:18,180
And his challenge in that sermon was that doing what is morally right

432
00:32:18,181 --> 00:32:24,240
is more important than proper politic as well as seeking to be safe.

433
00:32:24,800 --> 00:32:27,800
And it was something that resounded around the nation.

434
00:32:27,801 --> 00:32:33,880
It was a part of what was picked up as his challenge for America to be called to its moral conscience.

435
00:32:34,160 --> 00:32:39,760
And we were very pleased that he shared his last Sunday sermon here in this cathedral.

436
00:32:40,780 --> 00:32:45,140
But the National Cathedral is more than a forum to promote a moral imperative.

437
00:32:45,960 --> 00:32:49,540
It is also a facility for personal growth and reaffirmation.

438
00:32:50,760 --> 00:32:57,480
One of the traditions of great cathedrals is to advance and support education.

439
00:32:57,800 --> 00:33:04,800
And so you will find in many of the great cathedrals, especially in Europe, that there are schools that are attached.

440
00:33:05,360 --> 00:33:11,160
We have here in this cathedral, as a part of its foundation, three schools.

441
00:33:11,660 --> 00:33:15,280
The schools have about 1,500 students in them.

442
00:33:15,960 --> 00:33:18,220
But a cathedral is also about the future.

443
00:33:18,920 --> 00:33:23,300
It is about a vision of ministry that is continual.

444
00:33:23,301 --> 00:33:29,180
We envision the continuation of our ministry to the city and to the world.

445
00:33:29,680 --> 00:33:34,380
We are very committed to continue our outreach in areas such as literacy,

446
00:33:34,840 --> 00:33:39,940
lobbying for social justice, the feeding of the hungry, housing,

447
00:33:40,260 --> 00:33:42,980
things of this sort are a great concern for us.

448
00:33:43,760 --> 00:33:47,340
Perhaps it's not surprising that when people of goodwill gather together,

449
00:33:47,780 --> 00:33:50,380
their efforts speak to the better angels of our nature.

450
00:33:50,381 --> 00:33:55,760
Under the pinnacles of a Gothic cathedral, that dynamic seems to suggest the intimate

451
00:33:55,761 --> 00:33:58,780
connection between people and God.

452
00:34:00,820 --> 00:34:06,460
I like to think of the cathedral not so much as a convergence of heaven and earth, but

453
00:34:06,461 --> 00:34:12,720
I like to see it as the marriage of the human and the divine.

454
00:34:13,520 --> 00:34:18,300
If indeed we see the divine in humanity as creativity.

455
00:34:18,301 --> 00:34:23,880
And so therefore, in that sense, when I look at this cathedral and I look at its great

456
00:34:23,881 --> 00:34:29,620
pinnacles and I look at the carvings within it, and I have this sense, what faith inspired

457
00:34:29,621 --> 00:34:30,500
the craftsmen?

458
00:34:30,960 --> 00:34:37,780
What faith inspired the window painters and the masons?

459
00:34:38,420 --> 00:34:41,400
What inspired them to do such marvelous work?

460
00:34:41,401 --> 00:34:47,820
And I think that it has within me the kind of excitement that says it is inspired of

461
00:34:47,821 --> 00:34:52,700
God that we could create something that would be totally for the glory of God.

462
00:34:53,000 --> 00:35:00,140
So to me, it is the coming together of the divine in humankind in such a way that it

463
00:35:00,141 --> 00:35:03,480
produces a magnificent expression of our love to God.

464
00:35:07,760 --> 00:35:11,980
It was such a wonderful experience to be a part of the building of the cathedral and

465
00:35:11,981 --> 00:35:16,600
to create something worthy, worthy of the Lord and worthy of the best that this nation

466
00:35:16,601 --> 00:35:17,301
could offer.

467
00:35:17,820 --> 00:35:20,360
When you finish setting that last finial up there,

468
00:35:21,080 --> 00:35:23,880
I also thought of the hundreds of men I'd worked with,

469
00:35:24,180 --> 00:35:26,760
the architects who were gone, no longer here.

470
00:35:27,620 --> 00:35:30,540
I would have to say it was exhilaration, of course.

471
00:35:31,260 --> 00:35:32,840
It has to be exhilarating to do that

472
00:35:32,841 --> 00:35:38,320
and to have 17,000 people down there watching it be set and to realize.

